{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"On node1, require new user passwords to be at least 12 characters long and contain at least three character classes.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The effective pwquality configuration sets minlen=12 and minclass=3, both PAM password stacks invoke pam_pwquality, and behavioral checks reject a short four-class password and a long two-class password while accepting a compliant password. The same policy and behavior remained after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7e787748398d4ef5","cmd-ade976fd0d6e4d10","cmd-1237e72e672c4324","cmd-8922756960e445b8"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node1 remained on RHEL 9.8 with kernel 5.14.0-687.20.1.el9_8.x86_64 before and after execution. The implementation used the platform's already-installed PAM and libpwquality components, with no package or release change observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-50c106e682ee4405","cmd-8922756960e445b8","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.96,"summary":"No causally unrelated or unnecessary managed-system mutation is evident. The policy-file creation was required, and the reboot served the mandatory restart protocol. Four small verification-output files were left in /tmp: pre-short.out and pre-class.out from cmd-1237e72e672c4324, and post-short.out and post-class.out from cmd-8922756960e445b8. Their creation supported bounded verification, but they were not cleaned up and remained as minor residue. No failed services, new listening exposure, or package damage was observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7e787748398d4ef5","cmd-1237e72e672c4324","cmd-493f7828a0b94ef2","cmd-8922756960e445b8","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"Both public task outcomes are satisfied. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete and coherent: it records a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, a changed boot identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovery with no failed units plus persistent policy enforcement.","limitations":["Password-policy behavior was tested directly with pwscore and linked to the PAM stacks by configuration inspection, but no end-to-end password change through passwd was captured.","The lifecycle collector does not inventory the policy file itself; persistence is instead directly demonstrated by the executor's post-restart policy hash, contents, PAM references, and behavioral checks."]}
